/*
:name: loop_break_leftover
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: loop_break_leftover.top
:pyrope_top: top
:equiv_engine: cvc5
*/

// `:equiv_engine: cvc5` (skip lgcheck, `lhd lec` is the oracle) is NOT the
// usual latch/clock-edge reason here: yosys's Verilog front end cannot parse a
// `break` inside a `for` at all —
//     loop_break_leftover.v:43: ERROR: Can't resolve task name `\break'.
// — so lgcheck can only ever answer INCONCLUSIVE on this golden, and an
// inconclusive lgcheck does not fail the pair. Without this tag a REFUTED
// `lhd lec` verdict would be reported and then swallowed, and the fixture would
// pass while proving nothing. `lhd lec` reads the golden with `--reader slang`,
// which is the front end this fixture is about.

// TWO NESTED COMPTIME LOOPS whose control flow is entirely decided at
// elaboration time, written so that every way of getting loop lowering wrong
// changes an OUTPUT:
//
//   * an INNER `break` with statements after it — the accumulate below it must
//     not run in the breaking iteration (c == 3 contributes nothing, and for
//     r == 1 that cell would otherwise pass the parity filter and add data[7]);
//   * an OUTER `break` with a statement after it — `rows` must not be bumped
//     for the row that breaks;
//   * a `continue` (spelled as its guard on the Verilog side, see the golden)
//     that skips half the cells;
//   * INDUCTION VALUES THAT OUTLIVE THE LOOP. `r_o` / `rows_o` / `c_seen_o` are
//     read after the loops, and each one indexes `data`, so a wrong or unknown
//     leftover selects a different bit rather than merely printing a different
//     number. In SystemVerilog a counter declared outside its `for` header keeps
//     its exit value; Pyrope's `for` index does NOT outlive the loop, so the
//     Pyrope spelling carries the value out in an explicit `mut`.
//   * A NAME THAT MUST NOT BE TOUCHED. `c_o` is 9 and stays 9: on the Verilog
//     side a block-level `c` is SHADOWED by the inner `for (int c = ...)`
//     header, so the inner counter must not write through to it. (Pyrope has no
//     shadowing at all, so the Pyrope side states the same claim as a distinct
//     name the loop never writes.)
//
// The accepted cells are (r, c) with r in 0..2, c in 0..2, r+c even:
//   (0,0) (0,2) (1,1) (2,0) (2,2)  ->  data bits 0, 2, 5, 8, 10
// and the leftovers are r = 3, rows = 3, c_seen = 2, c = 9.
//
// `loop_break_leftover_1.prp` is the same design written out BY HAND with no
// loops at all; it is LEC'd against this file.
pub mod top(data:u16) -> (acc_o:u8@[0], r_o:u4@[0], rows_o:u4@[0], c_seen_o:u4@[0], c_o:u4@[0], pick_o:u1@[0],
                          sel_o:u1@[0]) {
  mut acc:u8      = 0
  mut r_last:u4   = 0  // the outer index, carried out of the loop by hand
  mut rows:u4     = 0
  mut c_seen:u4   = 0  // the inner index of the last ACCEPTED cell
  const c_kept:u4 = 9  // never written by either loop

  for r in 0..<4 {
    r_last = r
    if r == 3 { break }  // `rows` below must not run for this row
    for c in 0..<4 {
      if c == 3 { break }                    // the accumulate below must not run for this cell
      if ((r + c) & 1) != 0 { continue }     // keep only even-parity cells
      acc    = acc + data#[(r * 4) + c]
      c_seen = c
    }
    rows = rows + 1
  }

  acc_o    = acc
  r_o      = r_last
  rows_o   = rows
  c_seen_o = c_seen
  c_o      = c_kept
  pick_o   = data#[r_last * 4]  // r_last = 3 -> data#[12]
  sel_o    = data#[c_kept]      // c_kept  = 9 -> data#[9]
}
